hero='''<section class="case-hero"><p class="case-kicker">Engineering software / Python / Signal processing</p><h1>Orbit Analyzer<span>From shaft motion<br>to a traceable result.</span></h1><p class="case-lead">A desktop tool for importing vibration recordings, inspecting X/Y shaft motion and isolating the frequency band around running speed—with the checks needed to trust the calculation.</p><div class="case-tags"><span>CSV &amp; Excel</span><span>Tkinter interface</span><span>Zero-phase filtering</span><span>Verified numerical behavior</span></div>'''
stats='''<dl class="case-stats"><div><dt>32,769</dt><dd>samples in the reference record</dd></div><div><dt>6,400 Hz</dt><dd>verified acquisition rate</dd></div><div><dt>12 tests</dt><dd>numerical checks passed</dd></div><div><dt>2 views</dt><dd>plots and validation details</dd></div></dl>'''
summary=hero+'''<div class="case-actions"><a class="case-button" href="work-08.html">Read the complete project ↗</a><a href="#overview">Project at a glance ↓</a></div></section>'''+UI+stats+'''
<section class="summary-grid" id="overview"><div><p class="case-kicker">The problem</p><h2>An orbit is only as reliable as its processing.</h2><p>The original script used a band-stop filter where a band-pass was intended, calculated sampling rate from the final timestamp and offered limited input validation. The supplied files also carried different timestamp precision.</p></div><div><p class="case-kicker">The solution</p><h2>A complete analysis workflow.</h2><p>The refined application accepts CSV and Excel, checks timing, exposes filter settings, excludes edge transients and shows raw motion, the filtered band, a central revolution and the spectrum together.</p></div></section>
<section class="case-section"><p class="case-kicker">What I built</p><h2>Engineering calculations made inspectable.</h2><ul class="feature-list"><li><strong>Input handling.</strong> Worksheet selection, optional CSV headers, finite-value checks and explicit sampling-rate override.</li><li><strong>Signal processing.</strong> Linear detrending and zero-phase Butterworth band-pass filtering with second-order sections.</li><li><strong>Evidence.</strong> Input notes, RMS and peak-to-peak results, numerical tests and cross-file comparison.</li><li><strong>Outputs.</strong> Full-precision processed CSV, a JSON settings report, and PNG/PDF plots.</li></ul></section>
<section class="case-section case-callout"><p class="case-kicker">Measured outcome</p><h2>Verified against known signals and supplied recordings.</h2><p>All 12 numerical tests passed. With the verified 6,400 Hz clock, the CSV and Excel filtered outputs agree within 0.0000004 µm. This verifies processing consistency; it does not replace sensor calibration or establish a machinery diagnosis.</p><a class="case-button" href="work-08.html#verification">Explore the validation evidence ↗</a></section>'''
(OUT/'summary.html').write_text(page('Orbit Analyzer — Project summary','Python shaft-orbit analysis: CSV and Excel inputs, zero-phase filtering, visual inspection and verified calculations.',summary,True),encoding='utf-8')

sections=[('overview','Overview'),('data','Understanding the data'),('workflow','Using the application'),('processing','Processing method'),('implementation','Program structure'),('results','Results & exports'),('verification','Validation'),('limits','Interpretation & limits')]
toc='<aside class="case-toc"><p>IN THIS PROJECT</p><nav aria-label="Documentation sections">'+''.join(f'<a href="#{key}">{i:02} / {title}</a>' for i,(key,title) in enumerate(sections,1))+'</nav></aside>'
body=hero+'''<div class="case-actions"><a class="case-button" href="#workflow">Explore the workflow ↓</a><a href="summary.html">Read the short version ↗</a></div></section>'''+UI+stats+'<div class="case-layout">'+toc+'''<article>
<section class="case-section" id="overview"><p class="case-kicker">01 / Overview</p><h2>Making the full calculation visible.</h2><p>Orbit Analyzer plots one displacement channel against another to show the shaft’s measured motion. It is designed for paired X/Y vibration recordings and focuses on the band around the entered running speed, often called 1×.</p><p>The work combined a review of the original Python script, an audit of three input files, corrections to signal processing, a desktop interface and reproducible validation. The result connects the input recording to plots, metrics and exported settings in one workflow.</p><h3>What changed</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Original behavior and implemented correction</caption><thead><tr><th>Issue</th><th>Correction</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Band-stop rejected the desired component.</td><td>Band-pass retains the chosen frequency region.</td></tr><tr><td>A preceding low-pass attenuated the running-speed component.</td><td>Removed the extra stage; apply one documented band-pass.</td></tr><tr><td>Sampling rate depended on the final timestamp.</td><td>Use elapsed time across N − 1 intervals and validate spacing.</td></tr><tr><td>One revolution was truncated to an integer sample count.</td><td>Interpolate a central slice spanning exactly 1/frequency seconds.</td></tr><tr><td>No reliable way to distinguish stale or transient-dominated results.</td><td>Clear results after input changes and exclude filter-edge samples.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>

<section class="case-section" id="data"><p class="case-kicker">02 / Understanding the data</p><h2>Three files. One recording. Different clocks.</h2><p>Each row contains time in seconds, X displacement and Y displacement in the same unit. The reference workbook contains 32,769 paired samples over 5.12 seconds. The matching instrument export identifies a 6,400 Hz acquisition rate and displacement in micrometres.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Audit of the supplied inputs</caption><thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Rows</th><th>Finding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>OrbitData.xlsx</td><td>32,769</td><td>Uniform 6,400 Hz timing; preferred reference.</td></tr><tr><td>OrbitData.csv</td><td>32,769</td><td>12,898 nonincreasing time intervals; errors up to 0.005 s. Requires explicit clock reconstruction.</td></tr><tr><td>orbit_data.csv</td><td>32,768</td><td>First zero row omitted; 0.000156 s steps imply 6,410.2564 Hz.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The two CSV displacement arrays match after accounting for the missing first row. Their rounding differs from Excel by at most 0.00004988 µm. The workbook’s X/Y values match the corresponding instrument export to floating-point precision.</p><div class="case-note"><strong>Preserve the sample order.</strong> Sorting corrupted timestamps would change which samples are adjacent. The application instead offers an explicit sample-rate override when the acquisition clock and uninterrupted row order are known.</div><h3>Input contract</h3><p>Exactly three columns are required: time, X, Y. A single text header is optional. CSV delimiters may be commas, semicolons or tabs. Blank rows are ignored; partly empty rows, nonnumeric values, NaN and infinity are rejected. Excel inputs use a selectable worksheet and numeric cached cell values. At least 16 samples are required, with a longer record needed for the selected filter.</p><p>The first (0, 0, 0) sample is retained and flagged. The importer never silently sorts, fills or drops numeric samples. Original input files remain unchanged.</p></section>

<section class="case-section" id="workflow"><p class="case-kicker">03 / Using the application</p><h2>Load, configure, inspect, export.</h2><ol class="workflow-list"><li><h3>Open the recording</h3><p>Choose CSV or XLSX. For Excel, select the relevant worksheet. Start with the reference workbook for this dataset.</p></li><li><h3>Confirm the analysis settings</h3><p>Enter the shaft frequency, low and high cutoffs, filter order and displacement unit. The reference settings are 24.97 Hz, a 22–28 Hz band, order 2 and µm. The unit field labels the data; it does not convert values.</p></li><li><h3>Resolve timing only when justified</h3><p>Normally, timing comes from the file. For the damaged CSV, enable Override clock and enter the independently verified 6,400 Hz rate. Time is reconstructed in existing row order and the decision is recorded in the report.</p></li><li><h3>Analyze and inspect both tabs</h3><p>Compare the raw orbit, valid filtered interior, central revolution and spectrum. Then read Data &amp; validation for timing checks, metrics, settings and limitations.</p></li><li><h3>Save a reproducible result</h3><p>Export the processed CSV and accompanying JSON report. Save plots as PNG or PDF. Changes to inputs invalidate the old result and disable export until the analysis is run again.</p></li></ol><h3>Reading the plot view</h3><p><strong>Raw orbit:</strong> the original X/Y relationship, including offsets and all recorded components. <strong>Filtered orbit:</strong> the selected band after detrending, with edge samples excluded. <strong>One revolution:</strong> a central time slice at the entered speed, with a start marker and direction arrow. <strong>Spectrum:</strong> a Hann-windowed periodogram of each raw channel, with the chosen band highlighted.</p>'''+CHECKS+'''<h3>When the application stops an analysis</h3><p>The interface explains repeated or backward timestamps, excessive interval variation, invalid frequencies and records too short for the selected band. Frequency settings must satisfy 0 &lt; low cutoff &lt; shaft frequency &lt; high cutoff &lt; sample rate / 2. The prototype order must be an integer from 1 to 6.</p></section>

<section class="case-section" id="processing"><p class="case-kicker">04 / Processing method</p><h2>Stable filtering, with time kept explicit.</h2><h3>Sampling and frequency resolution</h3><p>The sample interval is elapsed time divided by N − 1. Its reciprocal is the sampling rate. Individual time intervals must be positive and stay within 1% of the mean interval, allowing small timestamp rounding without accepting gaps or clock jumps.</p><p>Sample span is (N − 1) × dt; FFT record duration is N × dt; FFT bin spacing is sample rate / N. Keeping these quantities separate avoids an off-by-one timing error.</p>'''+TIMING+'''<h3>Detrending and the 1× band</h3><p>Both displacement channels are linearly detrended. A Butterworth band-pass is designed as second-order sections and applied forward and backward. This removes phase delay from the filter while squaring its single-pass amplitude response. The specified band edges therefore have approximately half-amplitude gain after both passes.</p>'''+FILTER+'''<h3>Excluding edge transients</h3><p>The largest filter-pole magnitude determines how long a response takes to decay to 0.1% amplitude. That interval is used for padding and excluded at each end. The code requires enough remaining samples for a complete revolution. This is a conservative transient heuristic, not a universal error bound.</p><p>The original 24.95–25 Hz band is far too narrow for the five-second recording under this criterion. With the new order-2 filter it needs over approximately 124.5 seconds. The broader 22–28 Hz default is an explicit choice, not an exact single-tone extraction.</p><h3>One revolution and metrics</h3><p>A central slice is interpolated to span exactly 1 / entered frequency seconds. RMS and peak-to-peak metrics are calculated across the entire valid filtered interior, not only that slice. Equal X/Y axis scaling preserves the plotted geometry.</p></section>

<section class="case-section" id="implementation"><p class="case-kicker">05 / Program structure</p><h2>A processing core behind a focused interface.</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Responsibilities without exposing the full implementation</caption><thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Responsibility</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Functions.py</td><td>File loading, worksheet discovery, timing validation, filter design, analysis, one-revolution extraction and CSV export.</td></tr><tr><td>Orbit.py</td><td>Tkinter controls, plot canvas, validation tab, file dialogs and result lifecycle.</td></tr><tr><td>test_orbit.py</td><td>Synthetic-signal recovery, invalid-input cases, timing and export round-trip tests.</td></tr><tr><td>validate_supplied.py</td><td>Reproducible audit and numerical comparison of the three supplied inputs.</td></tr><tr><td>ui_smoke.py</td><td>Actual Tk callback checks for loading, analysis, errors, clock override, stale results and export.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Technology:</strong> Python, NumPy for arrays and numerical operations, SciPy for signal processing, Matplotlib for plots, Tkinter for the desktop interface and openpyxl for Excel reading.</p><h3>Running the desktop program</h3><p>On the configured workstation, the Start Orbit Analyzer launcher opens the application. A fresh installation needs Python with Tcl/Tk and the dependencies in the project’s requirements file. A local virtual environment keeps those packages isolated. The interface starts only when the application is run, not when its module is imported.</p><p>The source snapshots above intentionally show only a small selection of implementation lines. This case study documents behavior and design; it does not distribute the full source or the input recordings.</p></section>

<section class="case-section" id="results"><p class="case-kicker">06 / Results &amp; exports</p><h2>A result you can inspect and reproduce.</h2><p>The following results use the reference workbook, 24.97 Hz entered speed, a 22–28 Hz band and order 2. Displacements are in micrometres.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Reference analysis results</caption><thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sampling rate / sample span</td><td>6,400 Hz / 5.12 s</td></tr><tr><td>FFT bin spacing</td><td>0.19530654 Hz</td></tr><tr><td>Dominant raw bin, X and Y</td><td>24.99923708 Hz</td></tr><tr><td>Excluded interval at each end</td><td>0.56671875 s</td></tr><tr><td>Valid interior samples</td><td>25,515</td></tr><tr><td>X / Y filtered RMS</td><td>2.56306744 / 7.20873529 µm</td></tr><tr><td>X / Y filtered peak-to-peak</td><td>8.02011305 / 20.90005668 µm</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>What each output contains</h3><ul class="feature-list"><li><strong>Processed CSV:</strong> effective time, raw X/Y, filtered X/Y and a valid-interior flag. Excluded edge samples remain available but are marked 0.</li><li><strong>JSON report:</strong> source and worksheet, unit label, analysis settings, metrics and notes, including an explicit clock override.</li><li><strong>PNG/PDF plot:</strong> the current orbit and spectrum figure for review or reporting.</li></ul><p>CSV output retains floating-point precision. The original data is not overwritten. Export is enabled only for the current analysis settings.</p></section>

<section class="case-section" id="verification"><p class="case-kicker">07 / Validation</p><h2>Checked against signals with known answers.</h2><p>Twelve automated numerical tests passed. Synthetic X/Y sinusoids were combined with offsets, linear drift and out-of-band interference, allowing comparison with a known orbit rather than relying on appearance alone.</p><ul class="feature-list"><li><strong>Signal recovery:</strong> relative RMS error below 0.5% for the synthetic case.</li><li><strong>Independent projection:</strong> recovered amplitudes within 0.2%; phase coefficients within 0.005 displacement units.</li><li><strong>Timing and boundaries:</strong> timestamp offsets, irregular or nonincreasing time, invalid cutoff/order, short records and exact revolution duration checked.</li><li><strong>Input and export:</strong> malformed cells, optional headers, delimiters, explicit clock reconstruction and full-precision round-trip checked.</li><li><strong>Cross-file consistency:</strong> using 6,400 Hz for the damaged CSV produces filtered results within 3.852 × 10<sup>−7</sup> µm of the workbook result.</li><li><strong>Desktop workflow:</strong> actual callback tests cover CSV/XLSX loading, analysis, error reporting, override, stale-state prevention, exports and plot generation. Visual inspection confirmed plots, text and controls.</li></ul><p>Validation was completed on November 18, 2026. These checks establish numerical behavior for the documented cases and processing assumptions.</p></section>

<section class="case-section" id="limits"><p class="case-kicker">08 / Interpretation &amp; limits</p><h2>What the output can—and cannot—tell us.</h2><p>The orbit shows the relationship between the two measured displacement channels. Physical shaft geometry requires appropriately calibrated, synchronized probes and known probe orientation. No probe-angle correction or tachometer phase reference is present in the supplied three-column files.</p><p>The one-revolution view is a time slice, not a tachometer-synchronized orbit or synchronous average. The dominant FFT bin is not a precision speed measurement: roughly 0.1953 Hz bin spacing cannot independently confirm 24.97 Hz to two decimal places. A fixed band also assumes the entered speed remains meaningful over the record.</p><p>Filtered metrics depend on the selected band and its amplitude response. The application supports inspection and traceable calculation; these results alone do not establish a machinery fault or replace instrument calibration.</p><h3>Method references</h3><p><a href="https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.signal.butter.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SciPy Butterworth filter design ↗</a><br><a href="https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.signal.sosfiltfilt.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SciPy forward/backward SOS filtering ↗</a></p><div class="case-actions"><a class="case-button" href="summary.html">Back to project summary ↗</a><a href="../../../index.html#portfolio">Explore more projects</a></div></section>
</article></div>'''
(OUT/'work-08.html').write_text(page('Orbit Analyzer — Full project documentation','Complete Orbit Analyzer case study: data audit, desktop workflow, filtering corrections, selected code snapshots, outputs and validation.',body),encoding='utf-8')
